curate.\n\nNow I'm setting up the file type color mapping. I'll define named constants for each file type's ANSI color code rather than scattering raw values throughout the code, following the style guide's preference for centralized constants over bare literals. Finishing the file type color matching logic...\n\nThe switch statement maps different file modes to their corresponding ANSI color codes—directories get blue, symlinks cyan, character devices get bold yellow, sockets magenta, pipes yellow, and executable regular files turn green. For regular files without execute permissions, I'm returning the reset code, which aligns with the requirement that regular files should display in default terminal color.\n\nNow I'm thinking through how this plays out when the color code gets used—if we're printing a regular filename with the reset sequence before and after it, that's technically redundant since we're already at default, but it's still correct and consistent with how the other file types work.\n\nFor the column layout with row-major distribution, I need to make sure the width calculation accounts for how entries map to columns—each entry at position `i` goes to column `i % numCols`, so the column widths are determined by taking the maximum display width across all entries that fall into that column, even if the last row is incomplete. I see the issue now—when there are fewer entries than requested columns, the algorithm still adds spacing gaps for the empty columns, inflating the total width artificially. So with 2 entries and 5 columns requested, it calculates width as if all 5 columns exist with gaps between them, even though the last 3 columns are empty and contribute nothing. This means it might incorrectly accept numCols=5 when the actual layout only uses 2 columns, defeating the purpose of the column-fitting logic. I'm realizing that increasing the number of columns doesn't necessarily increase total width in a row-major layout—it depends on how entries dis